## The shelf recipes: the name, the description, the model notes.
recipe-sea-water = Sea water
recipe-sea-water-description-novice = Water with salt dissolved in it — about 35 g per kilogram. You can "make" it yourself: distilled water + table salt. Boil the water away and the salt stays behind.
recipe-sea-water-description-school = Ocean salinity averages 3.5 %. Salt lowers the vapour pressure of water, so sea water boils a little above 100 °C and freezes at about −2 °C — gradually: fresh ice freezes out first and the remaining brine gets saltier.
recipe-sea-water-note = Real sea salt is not only NaCl (≈ 86 % Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions, the rest magnesium, sulfates, etc.). The model treats all of it as table salt.
recipe-salt-water-10 = Salt water 10 %
recipe-salt-water-10-description-novice = Water with table salt dissolved in it (10 g of salt per 100 g of solution). Salt water freezes at a lower temperature than fresh water — that is why roads are salted in winter.
recipe-salt-water-10-description-school = A 10 % NaCl solution starts to freeze at about −6.6 °C. The more salt, the lower the freezing point — down to −21.1 °C at 23.3 % salt: below that the brine freezes completely.
recipe-salt-water-20 = Salt water 20 %
recipe-salt-water-20-description-novice = Very salty water (20 g of salt per 100 g of solution). It starts to freeze only at about −16 °C and freezes completely only below −21 °C — an ordinary −18 °C freezer freezes just part of it.
recipe-salt-water-20-description-school = A 20 % NaCl solution starts to freeze at about −16.4 °C. The more ice has frozen out, the saltier the brine and the colder it takes, down to −21.1 °C.
